# QP5 - QP-10 through QP-13

Java 17+, standard libraries only. INSERT, UPDATE, transaction coordination, and
index execution contain working QP5 logic. SQL is parsed by the existing QO;
there is no canned SQL-to-plan stub or duplicate Value/Expression/RID definition.
All changes in this contribution are confined to `query-processor`.

## Run

From the repository root in PowerShell:

```powershell
./query-processor/run-tests.ps1
```

Use `-JdkHome 'C:\path\to\jdk'` to choose a JDK explicitly. The script compiles
existing QO/CCM sources and QP sources with `--release 17 -Xlint:all -Werror`.
Build outputs and fixture snapshots go into the system temporary directory.
Failed compilation or failed checks return a nonzero result. No commit/push is performed.

## Files and boundaries

| Location | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `src/main/java/sqlike/qp/` | Six QP5 classes: Insert/Update/CreateIndexExecutor and Transaction/Access/MutationCoordinator. |
| `IndexScanOperator.java` | QP-13 index lookup then RID fetch; coordinate ownership with QP3 before integration. |
| `port/QpContracts.java` | Local QP contracts around existing shared types, plus missing dependency interfaces. These are provisional contracts, not stub behavior. |
| `port/StorageAdapter.java` | Adapts the QP storage API to the existing `sqlike.contract.IStorageManager`. |
| `src/stubs/.../StubRuntime.java` | STUB ONLY: QP1 session, QP2 bases/expression evaluator, and QP3 target collector. |
| `src/stubs/.../StubQueryRunner.java` | STUB ONLY: limited single-table QP1/QP2/QP3 wiring, filters/projection, and result formatting. Calls existing QO for arbitrary supported SQL. |
| `src/stubs/.../StubStorage.java` | STUB ONLY: functional SM fake with transaction overlays, file snapshots, and map-backed indexes. |
| `src/stubs/.../StubTransactions.java` | STUB ONLY: combined CCM/FRM fake with IDs/states, scripted grants/WAIT/denial, event traces, permits, and rollback. |
| `src/test/.../Qp5AcceptanceTest.java` | Nine focused QP-10 through QP-13 acceptance scenarios, including separate-process restart. |

Thin wrappers bridge local QP types such as
`Row`, `Schema`, `Expr`, and `Rid` to existing shared `Value`, `TableSchema`,
`Expression`, and `RID`. No files in QO, CCM, SM, or FRM were edited/copied.

## Execution rules

- QP1 must serialize each session's commands. The runner demonstrates begin/ensure,
  access acquisition, execution/resource cleanup, implicit completion, runtime
  failure rollback, and disconnect cleanup. The real QP1 server/CLI is not supplied.
- Access is table scoped. WRITE permits the reads needed by UPDATE. CCM owns actual
  locking and waiting policy; `waitAccess` should block/notify rather than busy-spin.
- INSERT reserves a RID before authorizing its change. Every mutation sends full
  before/after images through CCM before applying it with an LSN and apply lease.
  QP never writes WAL or performs physical rollback itself.
- UPDATE materializes/deduplicates targets before writes and calculates one SET
  column from each original row. Counts represent values actually changed; assigning
  the existing value reports zero. Integer-to-FLOAT assignment is allowed; incompatible
  types/columns fail. The functional evaluator stub handles the QO expression tree.
- Only COMMITTED/ABORTED clear session state. Rejected/incomplete completion retains
  the ID for ABORT/retry. Auto-commit rejection is an error. Runtime failures abort
  the whole active transaction through CCM. Syntax/plan failures before execution
  do not silently replace an explicit transaction.
- Index creation delegates type/table/column to SM. IndexScan calls lookup then
  RID fetch and never falls back to scan. Automatic index choice remains QO's job.

## What the checks prove

Nine scenarios cover all 16 acceptance bullets of QP-10 through QP-13. Each scenario
has a named method and explicit result checks. Supported SQL is processed by the
actual parser and optimizer facade in this branch. The UPDATE persistence check
launches distinct writer/reader JVMs and verifies committed arithmetic results
from disk. The test file has no separate demo or unrelated regression scenarios.

Passing means component acceptance with explicitly substituted remaining services.
It does not certify full-system ACID/WAL, real SM pages/B+Tree, or multi-client
isolation. The existing QO Binder/CostModel and parts of access-path optimization
are still placeholders; this contribution does not claim to complete them.
IndexScan is tested with an actual QO `PlanNodes.indexScan` plan because automatic
index selection is not available yet. The real CLI's rendering is represented by
the runner's tested result messages.

Stub SM persists committed overlays through a flushed, atomically replaced fixture
snapshot and a transaction-sequence sidecar. Rollback discards only that transaction's
overlay. HASH uses maps; BPLUS_TREE range behavior examines indexed keys in a map,
not a B+Tree algorithm. Only committed index definitions are searchable in this fake.
Snapshots use bounded Java `writeUTF` strings. No persistent WAL/crash recovery is
implemented. The optional `TransactionScope` adapter hook exists only for fixture
overlays; a real SM uses the agreed concurrency/recovery protocol.

Before integration, reconcile the provisional QP concurrency port with CCM's final
API, replace the four explicit stub files with the owning teams' implementations,
and coordinate IndexScan with QP3. Re-run these scenarios against real dependencies.
This submission does not complete the whole QP, reports, or a milestone release.
